WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.360
Hey, welcome back.

00:00:01.980 --> 00:00:05.920
A magnetic field can exist around a circuit without producing any induced

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:06.280
current.

00:00:06.860 --> 00:00:08.780
The key question is what changes.

00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:13.280
Before we answer it, let's build the quantity that tracks the field through a

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:14.560
loop: magnetic flux.

00:00:15.460 --> 00:00:18.920
Choose a surface spanning the loop and an arrow perpendicular to it.

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:20.900
That arrow is the surface normal.

00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:26.720
For a uniform field and a flat surface, flux equals field strength times area

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:29.780
times cosine of the angle between the field and the normal.

00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:31.520
Not the angle with the plane.

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:34.160
That little distinction does a lot of work.

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:38.040
When the field follows the normal, flux is positive B A.

00:00:38.740 --> 00:00:40.920
When it lies in the plane, flux is zero.

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:45.980
Reverse the chosen normal and the signed flux reverses, without changing the

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:47.020
physical field.

00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:51.735
Try a uniform field of zero point four tesla through an area of zero point

00:00:51.735 --> 00:00:51.973
zero

00:00:51.973 --> 00:00:52.840
two square meters.

00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:56.040
The field makes sixty degrees with the normal.

00:00:56.540 --> 00:00:57.440
Pause and find the flux.

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:02.760
It is zero point zero zero four weber, because cosine sixty is one half.

00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:07.420
If sixty degrees were measured from the plane instead, the angle with the

00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:08.740
normal would be thirty degrees.

00:01:09.260 --> 00:01:11.220
One weber is one tesla square meter.

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:16.520
For a nonuniform field, add the contributions of small area elements using the

00:01:16.520 --> 00:01:17.020
flux integral.

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:22.560
Also, zero net magnetic flux through a closed surface does not mean zero flux

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:24.380
through the open surface bounded by a loop.

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:28.820
Now we can ask the right question: how fast does the flux linkage change?

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:33.780
For a coil with N identical turns, each linking the same flux, flux linkage is

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:35.380
N times the flux through one turn.

00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:40.560
Faraday's law says the induced electromotive force, or emf, is minus the time

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:41.560
derivative of that linkage.

00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:45.240
Despite the name, emf is measured in volts, not newtons.

00:01:45.740 --> 00:01:49.300
The minus sign contains direction information; we will unpack it next.

00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:53.300
For magnitude, faster flux change means greater induced emf.

00:01:53.620 --> 00:01:58.200
A large constant flux gives zero induced emf in a stationary, unchanged loop.

00:01:59.320 --> 00:02:02.140
Field strength, loop area or orientation can change the linkage.

00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:05.600
Our first example keeps area and orientation fixed.

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:10.080
An eighty turn coil has area zero point zero zero five square meters per turn.

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:15.400
A uniform field perpendicular to its plane rises by zero point three tesla in

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:17.740
zero point two seconds, at a constant rate.

00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:20.340
The rate is one point five tesla per second.

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:22.880
Multiply eighty by area by that rate.

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:26.120
The induced emf magnitude is zero point six volt.

00:02:26.860 --> 00:02:30.700
In a closed circuit with total resistance six ohms, the current magnitude is

00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:33.920
zero point one ampere, assuming inductive effects are negligible.

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:38.460
An open circuit can have induced emf without a circulating conduction current.

00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:42.720
If turns link different fluxes, add their individual linkages instead of

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:44.680
automatically writing N times one flux.

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:49.200
Separate the flux calculation, the rate calculation and the circuit response.

00:02:49.512 --> 00:02:52.072
Let's turn the minus sign into a picture.

00:02:53.312 --> 00:02:53.792
Face the page.

00:02:54.512 --> 00:02:58.712
A stationary conducting loop lies in the page, and the external magnetic field

00:02:58.712 --> 00:02:59.572
points into it.

00:03:00.572 --> 00:03:02.052
Crosses represent into the page.

00:03:02.992 --> 00:03:04.592
Suppose that inward field is increasing.

00:03:05.472 --> 00:03:07.012
What field would oppose the increase?

00:03:07.932 --> 00:03:08.472
Take a moment.

00:03:09.012 --> 00:03:10.212
An outward induced field.

00:03:10.972 --> 00:03:14.252
Curl the fingers of your right hand in the current direction so the thumb

00:03:14.252 --> 00:03:15.312
points out toward you.

00:03:15.952 --> 00:03:20.467
The induced conventional current is counterclockwise, as viewed facing the

00:03:20.467 --> 00:03:20.772
page.

00:03:21.852 --> 00:03:25.652
Now keep the external field pointing inward, but let its magnitude decrease.

00:03:26.472 --> 00:03:27.952
Does the current stay counterclockwise?

00:03:28.772 --> 00:03:29.132
No.

00:03:29.672 --> 00:03:32.652
The response now tries to maintain the disappearing inward flux.

00:03:33.352 --> 00:03:36.192
The induced field points inward, so the current is clockwise.

00:03:37.232 --> 00:03:41.372
The same original field direction gave opposite current directions because the

00:03:41.372 --> 00:03:42.052
change reversed.

00:03:43.012 --> 00:03:47.052
Lenz's law opposes the change in magnetic flux, not always the magnetic field

00:03:47.052 --> 00:03:47.472
itself.

00:03:48.192 --> 00:03:50.112
Choose the normal out of the page.

00:03:51.172 --> 00:03:55.153
Increasing inward flux becomes more negative, so its derivative is negative

00:03:55.153 --> 00:03:55.312
and

00:03:55.312 --> 00:03:58.172
induced emf is positive in the counterclockwise sense.

00:03:59.312 --> 00:04:01.672
Decreasing inward flux gives the opposite signs.

00:04:02.712 --> 00:04:06.152
Reversing the normal reverses both mathematical conventions, not the real

00:04:06.152 --> 00:04:06.432
current.

00:04:07.272 --> 00:04:09.932
The induced field need not cancel the external field completely.

00:04:10.772 --> 00:04:13.892
In a resistive circuit, its size follows the circuit response.

00:04:14.552 --> 00:04:18.872
Lenz's law also expresses energy conservation: induction does not supply

00:04:18.872 --> 00:04:20.712
unlimited electrical energy for free.

00:04:21.932 --> 00:04:25.312
The source changing the field or moving the apparatus must account for the

00:04:25.312 --> 00:04:25.912
energy transferred.

00:04:26.568 --> 00:04:32.028
A flux time graph is a shortcut, provided you know what the vertical axis

00:04:32.028 --> 00:04:32.468
means.

00:04:33.408 --> 00:04:37.228
Our graph shows signed flux through one turn of a fifty turn coil.

00:04:38.668 --> 00:04:41.108
The normal and positive circulation direction are fixed.

00:04:42.148 --> 00:04:46.748
First, flux rises linearly from zero to zero point zero zero six weber in zero

00:04:46.748 --> 00:04:47.508
point three seconds.

00:04:48.248 --> 00:04:51.488
Its slope is positive zero point zero two weber per second.

00:04:52.488 --> 00:04:53.988
Multiply by minus fifty.

00:04:54.628 --> 00:04:56.468
The induced emf is minus one volt.

00:04:57.848 --> 00:05:02.028
Next comes a plateau: the flux stays at zero point zero zero six weber from

00:05:02.028 --> 00:05:03.968
zero point three to zero point six seconds.

00:05:05.388 --> 00:05:06.448
What is the emf there?

00:05:07.188 --> 00:05:08.648
Think slope, not height.

00:05:09.088 --> 00:05:09.568
It is zero.

00:05:10.068 --> 00:05:11.828
A nonzero flux is not enough.

00:05:12.888 --> 00:05:16.648
Finally, flux falls linearly to zero over the next zero point six seconds.

00:05:17.128 --> 00:05:21.828
The slope is minus zero point zero one weber per second, so emf is plus zero

00:05:21.828 --> 00:05:22.608
point five volt.

00:05:23.768 --> 00:05:27.588
The return takes twice as long as the rise, so the magnitude is half as large.

00:05:28.308 --> 00:05:31.148
The signs refer to our chosen positive circulation direction.

00:05:32.188 --> 00:05:36.048
If the graph instead showed total flux linkage, we would not multiply by N

00:05:36.048 --> 00:05:36.348
again.

00:05:37.068 --> 00:05:40.744
At ideal sharp corners, a unique derivative is not defined; use the

00:05:40.744 --> 00:05:41.348
appropriate

00:05:41.348 --> 00:05:44.908
open time interval rather than assigning both slopes to one instant.

00:05:45.988 --> 00:05:47.628
Real transitions are smoothed.

00:05:48.408 --> 00:05:52.288
Sketching the corresponding emf graph gives three constant levels: minus one,

00:05:52.648 --> 00:05:54.288
zero and plus one half volt.

00:05:54.576 --> 00:05:59.276
For a curved flux time graph, average and instantaneous emf can differ.

00:06:00.096 --> 00:06:02.756
Let's solve one clean JEE style problem.

00:06:03.176 --> 00:06:07.516
A fixed forty turn coil has area zero point zero three square meters per turn.

00:06:08.256 --> 00:06:10.936
A uniform magnetic field follows its chosen normal.

00:06:11.656 --> 00:06:15.516
Field strength as a function of time is zero point one plus zero point two

00:06:15.516 --> 00:06:18.816
times t squared, in tesla, with t in seconds.

00:06:19.576 --> 00:06:21.596
Find the induced emf at two seconds.

00:06:22.336 --> 00:06:25.316
First write the flux linkage: forty times area times B.

00:06:25.756 --> 00:06:28.336
The angle is zero, so cosine contributes one.

00:06:28.956 --> 00:06:30.736
Differentiate before substituting the time.

00:06:31.316 --> 00:06:34.776
The derivative of B is zero point four times t tesla per second.

00:06:35.756 --> 00:06:38.936
Multiply by minus forty times zero point zero three.

00:06:38.936 --> 00:06:41.556
The emf is minus zero point four eight times t volts.

00:06:42.296 --> 00:06:44.916
At two seconds, that is minus zero point nine six volt.

00:06:45.596 --> 00:06:49.536
With total resistance eight ohms and negligible inductive effects, current

00:06:49.536 --> 00:06:51.596
magnitude is zero point one two ampere.

00:06:52.176 --> 00:06:55.076
Its circulation is opposite our positive right hand convention.

00:06:55.976 --> 00:06:58.196
Now compare an interval from zero to two seconds.

00:06:58.936 --> 00:07:02.996
B changes by zero point eight tesla, giving an average rate of zero point four

00:07:02.996 --> 00:07:03.876
tesla per second.

00:07:05.076 --> 00:07:08.856
Average emf is minus zero point four eight volt, not the final instantaneous

00:07:08.856 --> 00:07:09.256
value.

00:07:10.196 --> 00:07:12.936
Constant initial field contributes flux, but no derivative.

00:07:13.856 --> 00:07:18.136
Before finishing any induction problem, check the normal, linked turns, the

00:07:18.136 --> 00:07:21.796
time dependence, your direction convention and whether the circuit model

00:07:21.796 --> 00:07:24.176
permits current equal to emf over resistance.
